WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:02.065
Cross-Text Connections

00:00:02.445 --> 00:00:05.338
One sentence per author. Then compare.

00:00:06.538 --> 00:00:07.536
How to spot it

00:00:07.936 --> 00:00:09.433
Text 1 and Text 2

00:00:09.983 --> 00:00:13.519
The question says “how would the author of Text 2 respond”

00:00:14.069 --> 00:00:17.234
It’s testing: tracking two viewpoints at once

00:00:18.434 --> 00:00:19.229
The method

00:00:19.629 --> 00:00:22.178
Step 1: Summarise Text 1

00:00:22.458 --> 00:00:23.477
One sentence.

00:00:24.027 --> 00:00:26.485
Step 2: Summarise Text 2

00:00:26.765 --> 00:00:27.784
One sentence.

00:00:28.334 --> 00:00:30.846
Step 3: Name the relationship

00:00:31.126 --> 00:00:34.984
Agree, disagree, partly agree, or adds a limit.

00:00:35.534 --> 00:00:38.140
Step 4: Answer the exact point

00:00:38.420 --> 00:00:40.179
What would Text 2 say about THAT?

00:00:40.729 --> 00:00:41.838
In a few words

00:00:41.988 --> 00:00:44.360
Summarise both. Name the link.

00:00:45.560 --> 00:00:46.922
Relationship clues

00:00:47.322 --> 00:00:48.750
“It is true that…, but…”

00:00:49.030 --> 00:00:50.089
Partly agrees.

00:00:50.639 --> 00:00:52.158
“Whatever X intended…”

00:00:52.438 --> 00:00:55.050
Concedes one point, shifts to another.

00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:56.737
Usually measured

00:00:57.017 --> 00:00:59.283
Text 2 rarely rejects everything.

00:00:59.833 --> 00:01:01.154
Facts vs cause

00:01:01.434 --> 00:01:04.309
Often agree on the data, disagree on why.

00:01:05.509 --> 00:01:06.549
Let’s try one

00:01:06.949 --> 00:01:07.787
Text 1

00:01:08.064 --> 00:01:14.102
Studies show that students who play musical instruments tend to earn higher math scores than students

00:01:14.102 --> 00:01:21.889
who don’t. Clearly, learning music improves mathematical ability, and schools should expand their

00:01:21.889 --> 00:01:23.738
music programs for this reason.

00:01:23.988 --> 00:01:24.854
Text 2

00:01:25.104 --> 00:01:29.199
It is true that young musicians often excel at math.

00:01:29.199 --> 00:01:35.289
But families who can afford instruments and lessons also tend to provide other advantages,

00:01:35.289 --> 00:01:38.759
such as tutoring and quiet study spaces.

00:01:38.759 --> 00:01:44.662
Without studies that account for these factors, we cannot say that music itself causes better math

00:01:44.662 --> 00:01:45.479
performance.

00:01:46.042 --> 00:01:52.157
Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim made in Text

00:01:52.157 --> 00:01:52.638
1?

00:01:53.288 --> 00:01:56.670
Text 1: music causes better math scores.

00:01:57.220 --> 00:02:02.845
Text 2: it’s true that musicians score higher, but we can’t say music is the cause.

00:02:03.395 --> 00:02:08.033
Text 2 agrees on the pattern, but questions the cause. Choice B.

00:02:09.233 --> 00:02:10.228
Common traps

00:02:10.628 --> 00:02:11.863
Total disagreement

00:02:12.143 --> 00:02:14.661
Usually they clash on one specific point.

00:02:15.211 --> 00:02:16.056
Wrong author

00:02:16.336 --> 00:02:17.810
Mixing up who said what.

00:02:19.010 --> 00:02:20.475
Cross-Text Connections

00:02:20.755 --> 00:02:23.127
Summarise both. Name the link.

00:02:23.677 --> 00:02:24.450
Spot it

00:02:24.650 --> 00:02:29.318
Text 1 + Text 2: “how would the author of Text 2 respond”

00:02:29.868 --> 00:02:30.575
Steps

00:02:30.775 --> 00:02:33.543
1. Sum up Text 1 in one sentence

00:02:33.823 --> 00:02:36.534
2. Sum up Text 2 in one sentence

00:02:36.814 --> 00:02:38.196
3. Name the link

00:02:38.476 --> 00:02:40.927
4. Answer the exact point asked

00:02:41.477 --> 00:02:42.230
Remember

00:02:42.430 --> 00:02:45.083
“It is true that…, but…” = partly agrees

00:02:45.363 --> 00:02:47.630
Text 2 rarely rejects everything

00:02:47.910 --> 00:02:50.741
Often: same facts, different cause

00:02:51.291 --> 00:02:51.896
Trap

00:02:52.096 --> 00:02:53.892
Assuming total disagreement

00:02:54.642 --> 00:02:58.337
